    topics: [
      {
        id: 'testing-pyramid', title: 'Testing Pyramid',
        content: 'The testing pyramid is a strategic framework for organizing your test suite. At the base are unit tests, which should make up about 70 percent of your tests. These test individual components, services, and pipes in isolation. They are fast to execute, easy to debug, and provide precise feedback about what broke. In the middle layer are integration tests, roughly 20 percent of your suite. These verify that multiple units work together correctly, such as a component communicating with its child components or a service calling another service. At the top are end-to-end tests, comprising about 10 percent. These simulate real user workflows through the entire application and are the slowest but most comprehensive. The key insight is that each layer serves a different purpose. Unit tests catch logic errors early and run quickly during development. Integration tests catch issues that emerge when components interact, such as incorrect dependency injection or data flow problems. E2E tests validate critical business workflows like login, checkout, and data submission. A well-balanced pyramid gives you fast feedback during development while still catching integration issues and validating user journeys. Avoid putting too many tests at the top of the pyramid, as E2E tests are expensive to maintain and slow to run.'
      },
      {
        id: 'setup', title: 'Testing Setup',
        content: 'Angular provides built-in testing support through the Angular CLI. When you generate a component, service, or module, Angular automatically creates a corresponding spec file. The testing framework uses Jasmine as the test runner and Karma as the test server. To run your tests, execute the ng test command which compiles your application, starts Karma, opens a browser, and runs all spec files. You can add the code-coverage flag to generate a coverage report showing which lines of code are exercised by your tests. The watch mode flag keeps the test runner active and re-runs tests whenever you save a file, providing rapid feedback during development. TestBed is the primary Angular API for testing. It creates a dynamic testing module that mimics an Angular module, configured specifically for your tests. You configure TestBed in the beforeEach block to set up the testing environment before each test runs. This includes declaring components, providing services, and importing necessary modules. After configuration, call compileComponents to process any asynchronous template and CSS compilation. Then create a component fixture using TestBed.createComponent, which returns a handle to the component and its template for testing.'
      },
      {
        id: 'basic-test', title: 'Writing Basic Tests',
        content: 'Every test in Jasmine follows the describe-it pattern. The describe block groups related tests under a descriptive name, typically the class or feature being tested. Inside describe, the it block defines an individual test case with a specific expectation. The beforeEach block runs before each it block to set up shared state, such as creating component instances or injecting services. The expect function is the assertion mechanism. It takes a value and chains it with a matcher. Common matchers include toBe for strict equality, toEqual for deep equality, toBeTruthy and toBeFalsy for boolean checks, toContain for substring or array membership, and toThrow for error verification. For Angular components, you create a TestBed configuration, build the component with TestBed.createComponent, and then test its behavior. The fixture object wraps the component and provides access to the component instance, the native element, and change detection. After setting inputs or triggering events, call fixture.detectChanges to trigger Angular change detection and update the DOM. For services, you inject them through TestBed.inject and call methods directly, verifying the return values with expect. This straightforward approach covers most testing scenarios in Angular applications.'
      }
    ]
