        id: 'network-mocking', title: 'Network Interception',
        content: 'Playwright intercepts network requests using page.route, which lets you modify or fulfill requests before they reach the server. Pass a URL pattern as a string or regex, and a handler function that receives the route object. Call route.fulfill to provide a custom response with a status code, content type, and body. This is useful for testing loading states, error handling, and edge cases that are difficult to reproduce with a real backend. You can also use route.continue to modify the request before forwarding it, or route.abort to simulate network failures. Network mocking enables deterministic tests that do not depend on backend availability or data state. Mock API responses with consistent data, and verify your UI renders correctly regardless of server conditions. For testing loading indicators, add a delay to the fulfilled response to simulate slow network conditions. Mock error responses by fulfilling with a 500 status code and error message, then verify your application displays appropriate error states. Combine network mocking with viewport configuration to test responsive behavior under different network conditions. Always clean up route handlers after tests to avoid intercepting requests in subsequent tests.'
