        content: 'Memory leaks occur when components or services fail to clean up resources like subscriptions, event listeners, and timers. In Angular, the most common leaks come from unsubscribed Observables and forgotten event listeners. Test for memory leaks by creating and destroying components repeatedly while monitoring heap size. Use performance.memory.usedJSHeapSize to track memory allocation before and after the test. Create the component, trigger its initialization, destroy it, and force garbage collection. Repeat this cycle fifty times and compare the final heap size to the initial size. Allow a tolerance of a few megabytes for normal heap fluctuations. For subscription cleanup, verify that ngOnDestroy calls unsubscribe on all active subscriptions. Spy on the destroy method and check that subscription.closed is true after destruction. For event listeners, verify that removeEventListener is called for every addEventListener. Test that components using takeUntil or async pipe properly complete their Observables on destroy. Use RxJS TestScheduler to verify Observable completion without real timers. Regularly run memory leak tests as part of your CI suite to catch leaks early. Profile your application with Chrome DevTools Memory tab to identify specific leak sources when tests fail.'
