    topics: [
      {
        id: 'testing-http-services', title: 'Testing HTTP Services',
        content: 'HTTP services are among the most common services in Angular applications. Testing them requires intercepting network calls and providing controlled responses. Configure TestBed with provideHttpClient and provideHttpClientTesting to set up the testing HTTP backend. Inject HttpTestingController to control intercepted requests. For GET requests, call the service method, subscribe to the observable, then use httpMock.expectOne with the expected URL. Verify the request method is GET, then flush mock data using req.flush. For POST requests, verify the request body contains the expected payload before flushing the response. For DELETE requests, verify the URL includes the correct resource ID. For requests with query parameters, use a predicate function in expectOne that matches the URL pattern. Test error responses by calling req.error with a status object instead of req.flush. Subscribe to the error callback and verify the service handles it correctly. Always call httpMock.verify in an afterEach block to ensure all expected requests were made. This pattern works for all HTTP methods and provides complete control over request and response handling. Test both successful responses and various error scenarios including network failures, timeouts, and server errors.'
      },
      {
        id: 'testing-stateful-services', title: 'Testing Stateful Services',
        content: 'Stateful services manage application state and expose methods that mutate that state. Testing them requires verifying state transitions and side effects. Start by testing the initial state. After creating the service, verify all properties are in their expected initial values. Then test each method that modifies state by calling it and asserting the new state. For services that manage collections, test adding items, updating items, removing items, and clearing all items. Verify that calculations like totals, counts, and filters produce correct results after each mutation. For services that expose state as Observables, subscribe to the state stream and verify emitted values match expectations. Collect emitted values in an array and assert against the expected sequence of states. Test concurrent operations by triggering multiple state changes rapidly and verifying the final state is correct. When services depend on other services, mock the dependencies and verify the service calls them correctly. Test that state changes trigger appropriate side effects like notifications or side effects. For services using signals, test that signal values update correctly after operations. Verify computed signals derive correct values from their dependencies. Test effects that respond to signal changes by checking their side effects.'
      },
      {
        id: 'testing-rxjs-services', title: 'Testing RxJS Services',
        content: 'Services that use RxJS operators require careful testing to verify that observable pipelines produce expected results. For debounced search, use fakeAsync and tick. Subscribe to the search results observable, trigger multiple search terms with tick calls between them, then verify only the final debounced result is emitted. For switchMap behavior, start a request, trigger a second request before the first completes, and verify only the second result is emitted. The first request should be cancelled. For combined observables, use the TestScheduler to create virtual time streams. Marble testing provides a visual way to describe observable sequences using dash and parenthesis notation. For error handling, use catchError and retry operators. Test that errors are caught and retried the expected number of times before being re-thrown. Verify that the observable completes or errors as expected. For async pipe integration, test that components using async pipe display data correctly by triggering the underlying observable and calling detectChanges. Test subscription cleanup by verifying that ngOnDestroy completes subscriptions. Use takeUntil with a destroy$ subject to automatically unsubscribe. Verify the destroy$ subject completes when the service or component is destroyed. This prevents memory leaks and ensures clean test execution.'
      }
    ]
