      {
        id: 'testing-component-output', title: 'Testing Component Outputs',
        content: 'Component outputs use EventEmitters to communicate with parent components. Testing outputs verifies that the correct events are emitted with the correct data at the right time. Start by spying on the EventEmitter using jasmine.spyOn. Pass the component instance and the output property name, then chain .and to access the spy configuration. Trigger the action that should cause the emission. This might be clicking a button, calling a method, or simulating a user interaction. After triggering, assert that the spy was called with the expected payload using toHaveBeenCalledWith. For multiple emissions, verify each call separately. Test that outputs are not emitted when they should not be. For example, if a delete button should be hidden in readonly mode, verify the output is not emitted when readonly is true. Test output timing with fakeAsync. If an output is emitted after an async operation, use tick to advance time and verify the emission happens at the correct moment. For outputs with complex payloads, verify the entire payload structure using toEqual. Test that outputs emit the correct references, not just correct values, especially when the output carries object references that should match specific instances. This ensures parent components receive the exact data they expect.'
      },
      {
        id: 'testing-component-input', title: 'Testing Component Inputs',
        content: 'Testing component inputs involves setting values and verifying the component renders correctly. For signal-based inputs, use the signal setter directly on the component instance. Call the signal as a function with the new value, then call fixture.detectChanges to trigger change detection. Query the rendered DOM to verify the output matches the expected display. For traditional decorator-based inputs, use fixture.componentRef.setInput which sets the value through Angular\'s input binding mechanism. Test input changes by setting a new value and calling detectChanges again. Verify the component updates its display to reflect the new input. This ensures the component reacts correctly to dynamic data. Test default values by creating the component without setting inputs and verifying the default display. Test edge cases like empty strings, null values, zero, and very long strings. Verify the component handles these gracefully without errors. For components with multiple inputs, test combinations. Set one input while keeping others at defaults, then change multiple inputs simultaneously. Verify the component renders correctly for each combination. Test that inputs are properly typed by passing values of the expected type. Verify computed properties derived from inputs produce correct values after input changes.'
      },
      {
        id: 'testing-host-bindings', title: 'Testing Host Bindings',
        content: 'Host bindings allow components to modify their host element attributes, classes, and styles. Testing these bindings ensures the component correctly interacts with its host element. To test host class bindings, set the controlling property and call fixture.detectChanges. Query the native element and check that the expected class is present or absent using classList.contains. For example, a highlight directive that adds a class when active should verify the class appears when active is true and disappears when active is false. For host style bindings, set the controlling property and verify the native element style has the expected value. Check backgroundColor, color, or other CSS properties that the component sets dynamically. For host attribute bindings, verify that attributes like aria-label, role, tabindex, and data attributes are set correctly. Use getAttribute to check attribute values and hasAttribute to verify presence or absence. Test host listeners by dispatching events on the native element. Create a KeyboardEvent for keydown, a MouseEvent for click, or a custom event. Spy on the component method that handles the event and verify it was called. Test that host bindings respond to property changes by updating the controlling property multiple times and verifying the host element updates accordingly. This ensures the component maintains its host element state correctly throughout its lifecycle.'
      }
